#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Limit Using L'Hopital's Rule Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee0f398f-597a-5b09-b835-c7b222771d92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259774} {:answer/id #uuid "47ea25ec-db6b-58be-a50e-d5eac4a24611" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee0f398f-597a-5b09-b835-c7b222771d92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259774} :answer-field/id #uuid "65b65e21-4124-599c-8ef5-38bd7add3afa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e257b16a-a775-5397-9d95-12edd8b09eb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-336903" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{(x+4)^{3}}{3\\sqrt[3]{x+5}-x-7}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=-4$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{(x+4)^{3}}{3\\sqrt[3]{x+5}-x-7} & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{{[(x+4)^{3}]}^{′}}{{[3(x+5)^{1/3}-x-7]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3(x+4)^{2}}{(x+5)^{-2/3}-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{3(x+4)^{2}}{(x+5)^{-2/3}-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{{[3(x+4)^{2}]}^{′}}{{[(x+5)^{-2/3}-1]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -4}\\frac{6(x+4)}{-\\frac{2}{3}(x+5)^{-5/3}} \\\\  & =-9{lim}_{x\\to -4}(x+4)(x+5)^{5/3} \\\\  & =-9(-4+4)\\,(-4+5)^{5/3} \\\\  & =-9\\cdot 0\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
